  "content": {
    "id": "v2-a57f0-p58-practice-slot-11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Show that $\\dfrac{d}{dx}(\\sin x\\cos x)=2\\cos^2x-1$, and hence find $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/3}\\cos^2x\\,dx$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{d}{dx}(\\sin x\\cos x)=\\cos^2x-\\sin^2x=2\\cos^2x-1$; $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/3}\\cos^2x\\,dx=\\dfrac\\pi6+\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}8$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x\\cos x)&=\\cos x\\cos x+\\sin x(-\\sin x)\\\\&=\\cos^2x-\\sin^2x\\\\&=\\cos^2x-(1-\\cos^2x)\\\\&=2\\cos^2x-1.\\end{align*}$$\nHence\n$$\\cos^2x=\\frac12\\left(\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x\\cos x)+1\\right).$$\nTherefore\n$$\\begin{align*}\\int_0^{\\pi/3}\\cos^2x\\,dx&=\\frac12\\left[\\sin x\\cos x+x\\right]_0^{\\pi/3}\\\\&=\\frac12\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\cdot\\frac12+\\frac\\pi3\\right)-0\\\\&=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{8}+\\frac\\pi6.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
